{"title":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis","subtitle":"Insulin deficiency, unopposed lipolysis, and the anion gap acidosis it produces — endocrine/critical care chalk talk","guideline_sources":["ADA Standards of Care 2025","ADA/EASD Consensus Report on Hyperglycemic Crises 2024 (Diabetes Care)"],"references":[{"id":1,"source":"Hyperglycemic Crises in Adults with Diabetes: A Consensus Report","year":2024,"society":"ADA/EASD","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dci24-0032","type":"guideline"},{"id":2,"source":"ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes—2025","year":2025,"society":"ADA","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc25-SINT","type":"guideline"},{"id":3,"source":"Hyperglycemic Crises in Adult Patients with Diabetes (Position Statement)","year":2009,"society":"ADA","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc09-9032","type":"guideline"},{"id":4,"source":"FDA Drug Safety Communication: SGLT2 inhibitors and ketoacidosis","year":2015,"society":"FDA","type":"guideline"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Physiology & Pathophysiology — Why Ketones Accumulate","minutes":"3 min","points":["DKA = absolute (or severe relative) insulin deficiency plus excess counterregulatory hormones (glucagon, catecholamines, cortisol, GH). Insulin normally suppresses hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes; without it, unrestrained lipolysis floods the liver with free fatty acids.","Glucagon excess (unopposed by insulin) drives hepatic carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1 (CPT-1) activity up, shuttling those fatty acids into mitochondria for beta-oxidation → acetyl-CoA → hepatic ketogenesis (beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate). This is a normal starvation pathway pushed into pathologic overdrive.","Insulin deficiency also unleashes hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis while peripheral glucose uptake in muscle/fat is blocked → severe hyperglycemia (usually >250 mg/dL, though euglycemic DKA occurs, see below).","Glucose above the renal threshold (~180 mg/dL) causes osmotic diuresis → profound total-body water and electrolyte losses (Na, K, phosphate, magnesium) despite a normal or even elevated serum K on initial labs, because acidosis and insulinopenia shift K out of cells even as total-body K is depleted.","Ketoacids (beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate) are strong acids that consume serum bicarbonate buffering capacity → high anion gap metabolic acidosis; Kussmaul respirations are the respiratory compensation (blowing off CO2 to raise pH)."],"mechanism":"The entire syndrome is one hormonal switch flipped the wrong way: insulin off, glucagon/catecholamines on. That single imbalance simultaneously turns on lipolysis, ketogenesis, gluconeogenesis, and osmotic diuresis — every downstream lab abnormality traces back to it.","teaching_pearl":"SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin, canagliflozin) can precipitate euglycemic DKA — the drug blocks renal glucose reabsorption so serum glucose stays deceptively low (<250 mg/dL) even while ketogenesis rages, because insulinopenia/relative deficiency and glucagon excess still occur. Perioperative fasting, low-carb diets, and illness are classic triggers [4].","board_tip":"Classic triggers to memorize: the 5 I's — Infection, Infarction (MI), Insulin non-adherence, Intoxication (alcohol/cocaine), and Iatrogenic (SGLT2i, steroids, antipsychotics). New-onset T1DM presenting as DKA is also common in adults, not just kids."},{"heading":"Clinical Findings & Workup","minutes":"2 min","points":["Diagnostic triad: hyperglycemia (usually >250 mg/dL, lower or normal in euglycemic DKA), metabolic acidosis (pH <7.3 or bicarbonate <18 mEq/L), and ketonemia/ketonuria (beta-hydroxybutyrate ≥3.0 mmol/L or moderate-large urine ketones) [1,3].","Exam: Kussmaul respirations, fruity (acetone) breath, diffuse abdominal pain/vomiting (can mimic an acute abdomen), dehydration, tachycardia, and altered mental status if severe.","Labs: BMP/CMP (Na, K, bicarbonate, BUN/Cr), venous blood gas (VBG is sufficient — arterial not required), serum or urine ketones, anion gap = Na − (Cl + HCO3), calculated/corrected sodium (add 1.6 mEq/L per 100 mg/dL glucose above 100), and a search for the precipitant (CBC with differential, UA, blood cultures, CXR, troponin, lipase as indicated).","Severity grading (ADA/EASD 2024) uses pH, bicarbonate, and mental status to stratify mild/moderate/severe — this determines insulin route and ICU-level monitoring needs [1]."],"mechanism":"","teaching_pearl":"Don't be fooled by a 'normal' or high potassium on initial BMP — acidosis drives K out of cells via H+/K+ exchange even though total-body K is markedly depleted from osmotic diuresis and vomiting. Never give insulin before confirming K is not already low.","board_tip":"Corrected sodium is a board favorite: true hyponatremia from hyperglycemia's osmotic pull of water out of cells must be distinguished from a 'real' sodium disturbance — always calculate it before interpreting Na level."},{"heading":"Treatment — Fluids, Insulin, Potassium, and the Transition Off","minutes":"3 min","points":["Fluids first: isotonic saline (0.9% NaCl) 15-20 mL/kg (roughly 1-1.5 L) in the first hour to restore intravascular volume and improve renal perfusion, which itself lowers glucose by improving urinary clearance and reduces counterregulatory hormone drive [1,2].","Check potassium before insulin. If K <3.3 mEq/L, hold insulin and replete K first — insulin drives K intracellularly via Na-K-ATPase stimulation and can precipitate life-threatening hypokalemia and arrhythmia. If K 3.3-5.3, add 20-30 mEq K to each liter of fluid. If K >5.3, hold K repletion and recheck [1,3].","Insulin: regular insulin IV infusion at 0.1 unit/kg/hr (or 0.14 unit/kg/hr without a bolus) is the mainstay per ADA/EASD 2024 [1]. Mechanism: insulin shuts off hepatic ketogenesis (suppresses CPT-1/lipolysis) and gluconeogenesis, and drives peripheral glucose uptake — this resolves the acidosis, not just the hyperglycemia. Continue the infusion until anion gap closes and ketosis resolves, not just until glucose normalizes.","Add dextrose (D5 or D10) to IV fluids once glucose falls to ~200-250 mg/dL, while continuing the insulin infusion — this allows insulin to keep suppressing ketogenesis without causing hypoglycemia (glucose is the target you titrate around, but ketone/gap closure is the actual therapeutic endpoint).","Bicarbonate is NOT routinely recommended (only considered if pH <6.9) — the acidosis corrects as ketogenesis shuts off and is largely self-limited with fluids/insulin; bicarbonate can worsen hypokalemia and paradoxical CNS acidosis [1,3].","Transition to subcutaneous insulin only once the gap has closed, bicarbonate ≥18, and the patient can eat — overlap the first subQ dose with the IV infusion by 1-2 hours (subQ insulin takes time to reach therapeutic levels) to avoid rebound ketoacidosis [1]."],"mechanism":"Insulin's job in DKA is fundamentally anti-lipolytic and anti-ketogenic — it re-suppresses hormone-sensitive lipase and hepatic CPT-1, cutting off the fatty-acid supply to ketogenesis. Correcting glucose is almost a side effect; the true endpoint of therapy is anion gap closure, which is why you keep the insulin drip running (with dextrose backup) even after glucose normalizes.","teaching_pearl":"If a patient is on an SGLT2 inhibitor and presents with euglycemic DKA, treat identically (fluids, insulin, K, dextrose once glucose falls) — do not withhold insulin just because glucose looks reassuring; the ketogenesis is the disease, not the glucose number [4].","board_tip":"Two board pitfalls: (1) starting insulin before checking/repleting a low K → arrhythmia; (2) stopping the insulin drip as soon as glucose normalizes without ensuring the anion gap has closed → rebound DKA. Insulin infusion continues until the gap closes, with dextrose added to prevent hypoglycemia."},{"heading":"Outcomes & Prognosis","minutes":"1 min","points":["Adult DKA mortality is low (<1%) in modern management when treated per protocol at experienced centers, but rises sharply with age, severe acidosis (pH <7.0), and coma on presentation [1].","Cerebral edema is the feared complication and is largely a pediatric/young-adult phenomenon (incidence <1% but mortality 20-50% when it occurs) — thought to relate to overly rapid osmotic shifts, so fluid correction is more conservative in children.","Recurrent DKA is most often driven by insulin non-adherence (often due to cost/access) or undiagnosed precipitating infection — addressing the underlying driver is as important as the acute metabolic correction [1]."],"mechanism":"","teaching_pearl":"Every DKA admission is an opportunity to identify and fix the precipitant (missed insulin doses, new SGLT2i, infection, first presentation of T1DM) — treating the acidosis without addressing the trigger guarantees a bounce-back.","board_tip":""}],"summary_points":["DKA is fundamentally an insulin-deficiency/glucagon-excess state: unrestrained lipolysis feeds hepatic ketogenesis via CPT-1, producing the anion gap acidosis — hyperglycemia is a parallel effect, not the core problem.","Diagnosis requires the triad: hyperglycemia (or euglycemia with SGLT2i), high anion gap metabolic acidosis, and ketonemia/ketonuria — always calculate the corrected sodium and anion gap.","Treatment sequence matters: fluids first, check/replete potassium before starting insulin, then IV regular insulin infusion (0.1 unit/kg/hr) with dextrose added once glucose falls to ~200-250, continued until the anion gap closes — not until glucose normalizes [1].","Bicarbonate is reserved for pH <6.9 only; routine use is not guideline-recommended and can worsen hypokalemia [1,3].","Most common pitfalls: starting insulin with an undiscovered low potassium, and stopping the insulin drip too early (glucose-normal but gap still open) causing rebound ketoacidosis.","SGLT2 inhibitors can cause euglycemic DKA — a normal glucose does not rule out DKA in a patient on these agents [4]."],"visual_memory_card":{"top_left":"No insulin → unopposed lipolysis feeds hepatic ketogenesis","top_right":"Hyperglycemia + high anion gap acidosis + ketonemia, Kussmaul breathing","bottom_left":"VBG + BMP + beta-hydroxybutyrate + anion gap + corrected Na","bottom_right":"IV fluids, then insulin infusion (shuts off ketogenesis) + K repletion, add dextrose"}}
